    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#napoleon-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii.jpg" alt="Napoleon III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexandre_Cabanel_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexandre Cabanel, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Italy grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italian-unification">Risorgimento</a>, the movement to unite a peninsula divided among Austrian provinces, the Papal States, the Bourbon Kingdom of the Two Sicilies and several duchies. Its driving force was the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardinia">Sardinia</a>, ruled from Turin by the House of Savoy and guided by its prime minister, Camillo Benso, Count of Cavour. In alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, Sardinia defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#austria">Austria</a> in 1859 at Magenta and Solferino and gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lombardy">Lombardy</a>; in 1860 plebiscites joined Tuscany, Parma, Modena and the Romagna, while Savoy and Nice were ceded to France as the price of French support. That May <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>’s Expedition of the Thousand landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sicily">Sicily</a> and overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> in the south, and Sardinian troops marched through the Papal Marche and Umbria to meet him. After further plebiscites, the first Italian parliament in Turin proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> on 17 March 1861. Cavour died that June. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#venetia">Venetia</a> was gained in 1866 through alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and on 20 September 1870, after the French garrison had left during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>, Italian troops entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> through the breach at Porta Pia. Rome became the capital in 1871, after interim spells in Turin and, from 1865, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#florence">Florence</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#milan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milan.jpg" alt="Milan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#milan">Milan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milan_Cathedral_from_Piazza_del_Duomo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jiuguang Wang, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The new kingdom of some 22 million people was overwhelmingly rural and deeply unequal between regions. Around three-quarters of Italians were illiterate in 1861, most spoke regional dialects rather than the Tuscan-based standard language, and the south suffered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latifundium">latifundia</a>, poverty and malaria. The gap between north and south, the so-called Southern Question, became a permanent theme of national politics. Industrialisation from the 1890s concentrated in the triangle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#milan">Milan</a>, Turin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genoa">Genoa</a>, where textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#steel">steel</a>, engineering and firms such as Fiat, founded in 1899, and Pirelli flourished, helped by hydroelectric power. Poverty drove mass emigration: between the 1870s and 1914 well over ten million Italians left, many for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brazil">Brazil</a>, and their remittances supported families at home. Relations with the Catholic Church were hostile after the seizure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#papacy">papacy</a> refused to recognise the kingdom and long discouraged Catholics from voting. Socialist parties, trade unions and peasant leagues grew around 1900. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fascism">Fascism</a> the regime pursued self-sufficiency through the Battle for Grain, land reclamation such as the draining of the Pontine Marshes, and, after the Depression, state rescue of banks and industry through the Istituto per la Ricostruzione Industriale (IRI), founded in 1933.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-adwa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa.jpg" alt="Battle of Adwa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Adwa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franko Khoury., PDM-owner</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>’s armed forces were built on the Piedmontese army, but early campaigns were disappointing: in 1866 Italy lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#austria">Austria</a> on land at Custoza and at sea at Lissa, gaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#venetia">Venetia</a> only because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> won the war. The army spent the 1860s suppressing brigandage in the south. Colonial expansion followed, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eritrea">Eritrea</a> organised as a colony in 1890 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protectorate">protectorate</a> over much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#somalia">Somalia</a>, but the attempt to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> ended in defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a> in 1896. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italo-turkish-war">Italo-Turkish War</a> of 1911–1912 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#libya">Libya</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dodecanese">Dodecanese</a>. Abandoning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aztec-empire">Triple Alliance</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, Italy joined the Entente under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#london">London</a> of April 1915 and entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-world-war">First World War</a> that May. Eleven costly offensives on the Isonzo were followed by the collapse at Caporetto in 1917 and recovery on the Piave, ending in victory at Vittorio Veneto in 1918; some 600,000 Italian soldiers died. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>’s regime conquered Ethiopia in 1935–1936, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#poison-gas">poison gas</a>, sent forces to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> and occupied Albania in 1939. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-world-war">Second World War</a>, from June 1940, Italian forces were defeated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#north-africa">North Africa</a>, lost ships at Taranto and Cape Matapan, and suffered heavy losses on the Don in 1942–1943.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#victor-emmanuel-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii.jpg" alt="Victor Emmanuel II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VictorEmmanuel2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eugène Disdéri, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom took over the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardinia">Sardinia</a>’s constitution, the Statuto Albertino of 1848, which created a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> with an appointed Senate and an elected Chamber of Deputies. Only about two per cent of the population could vote in 1861, and the franchise was widened in 1882 and to almost all adult men in 1912. Administration was centralised on the French model, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#prefect">prefects</a> governing the provinces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>. The liberal Right governed until 1876, after which the Left under Agostino Depretis practised <em>trasformismo</em>, absorbing opponents into shifting majorities. Francesco Crispi pursued a strong state and colonial expansion in the 1890s, and Giovanni Giolitti dominated the years 1901–1914 with social reforms and accommodation of moderate socialists. The kings were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a>, Umberto I, assassinated at Monza in 1900, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a>. After the March on Rome in October 1922 the king appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> prime minister. Following the murder of the socialist deputy Giacomo Matteotti in 1924, laws of 1925–1926 abolished opposition parties and a free press, creating a one-party dictatorship with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#grand-council-of-fascism">Grand Council of Fascism</a> at its centre and a secret police, the OVRA. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lateran-treaty">Lateran Treaty</a> of 1929 settled the Roman Question, and racial laws against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jews">Jews</a> followed in 1938.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#paris"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris.jpg" alt="Paris" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#paris">Paris</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Tour_Eiffel_vue_de_la_Tour_Saint-Jacques,_Paris_ao%C3%BBt_2014_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yann Caradec from Paris, France, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> became a model for nineteenth-century nationalism, and its heroes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> above all, were admired across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a>. The kingdom gave the peninsula a common state, army, school system and railway network, and slowly spread the standard Italian language, although regional divisions persisted. Italy was counted among the great powers from the late nineteenth century and sat among the victors at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paris">Paris</a> Peace Conference in 1919, where frustration over its gains fed the idea of a mutilated victory. Its greatest political export was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fascism">Fascism</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>’s regime, with its mass party, leader cult and corporatist claims, became a template for movements across Europe and influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lateran-treaty">Lateran Treaty</a> created the Vatican City State, which still exists. Culturally the period produced the operas of Giuseppe Verdi and Giacomo Puccini, the Futurist movement, Guglielmo Marconi’s radio and the educational method of Maria Montessori, while the racial laws of 1938 drove scientists such as Enrico Fermi into exile. Italian colonial rule left violent legacies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#libya">Libya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eritrea">Eritrea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#somalia">Somalia</a>, and the emigrant communities created by the liberal era remain a major part of the populations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brazil">Brazil</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#benito-mussolini"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini.jpg" alt="Benito Mussolini" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mussolini_mezzobusto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The monarchy fell because it had tied itself to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fascism">Fascism</a> and to a lost war. After the Allied landing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sicily">Sicily</a> in July 1943, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#grand-council-of-fascism">Grand Council of Fascism</a> voted against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> on the night of 24–25 July, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> dismissed and arrested him, appointing Marshal Pietro Badoglio. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#allies">Allies</a>, signed at Cassibile on 3 September and announced on 8 September 1943, was followed by the flight of the king and government from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> to Brindisi, leaving the army without orders as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a> occupied central and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>. German paratroopers freed Mussolini, who headed the Italian Social Republic in the north, while the royal government in the south declared war on Germany. Partisan resistance and the Allied advance liberated the north in April 1945, and Mussolini was executed by partisans on 28 April. Discredited, Victor Emmanuel III handed his powers to his son Umberto as lieutenant-general in 1944 and abdicated on 9 May 1946. In the institutional referendum of 2 June 1946, in which women voted nationally for the first time, about 54 per cent chose a republic, with the north voting strongly for it and the south largely for the monarchy. Umberto II left for exile in Portugal, and the constitution of the Italian Republic came into force on 1 January 1948.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Italy turned a divided peninsula into a single nation state and a great power, but its weak liberal institutions gave way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>’s Fascist dictatorship, the first of its kind, and its alliance with Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a> ended in defeat, civil war and a republic in 1946.</em></p>

